{"question_id": "54026fce", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on what's worked in our discussions, here are some ways to stay connected:\n\n- **Virtual coffee breaks** — schedule regular informal video calls for casual, watercooller-style chats, with ground rules like no work talk, keeping it light, respecting everyone's time, being present, using video, and rotating conversation starters.\n- **Pick a reliable, easy-to-use video conferencing platform** so the technical side never gets in the way.\n- **Set a regular schedule and communicate why attending matters**, while keeping it optional rather than a mandate.\n- **Organize virtual games or team-building exercises** like trivia or online escape rooms.\n- **Create or join informal chat channels** on Slack or Microsoft Teams for non-work conversations.\n- **Gauge interest first** — raise the idea in your next team meeting as a collaborative suggestion, or send a survey, before rolling anything out.\n\nSince you've already planned to bring up virtual coffee breaks at your next team meeting, framing it as a collaborative suggestion aligns well with the \"discuss and agree before introducing\" approach.", "n_records": 12, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1998, "completion_tokens": 932, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|work_from_home_preference", "self|remote_work_social_isolation", "self|virtual_coffee_break_interest", "self|team_meeting_plan", "self|virtual_coffee_break_ground_rules", "self|virtual_coffee_break_schedule", "self|video_conferencing_preference", "assistant|virtual_coffee_break_suggestion", "assistant|online_team_activities_suggestion", "assistant|social_channels_suggestion", "assistant|discuss_and_agree_suggestion", "assistant|coffee_break_ground_rules_suggestion"], "digest_bytes": 2129}
{"question_id": "09d032c9", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Memory doesn't record phone battery-saving settings, but based on your gear, here's what I can offer:\n\n- **Carry the power bank you already own** — it's new, so make sure it's in your bag daily, especially since you travel and need to keep tech accessories organized.\n- **If it's not enough, upgrade capacity** — your current options include 10,000mAh models that charge a phone up to 3 times: Jackery Titan S ($25), Aukey 10000mAh ($20), Eggtronic 10000mAh with USB-C ($30), or Anker PowerCore Fusion with USB-C ($35). The 5,000mAh models only give 1.5–2 charges (Anker PowerCore 5000, Mophie Powerstation Mini).\n- **Pair it with a cable organizer** — you already own a wireless charging pad, so a cable organizer (e.g., Anker's $6 nylon/velcro one holding up to 10 cables) keeps your charging setup tangle-free on the road.\n\nMemory only records your accessory ownership and power bank/cable options; it does not contain general battery-life tips like screen or app settings.", "n_records": 49, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 4798, "completion_tokens": 1116, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|portable_power_bank", "self|wireless_charging_pad", "self|apple_watch", "self|travel_habits", "self|tv_purchase_plan", "assistant|tech_organization_suggestion", "assistant|travel_case_option", "assistant|bellroy_tech_kit_pros", "assistant|bellroy_tech_kit_cons", "assistant|peak_design_tech_pouch_pros", "assistant|peak_design_tech_pouch_cons", "assistant|cable_organizer_option", "assistant|power_bank_option", "assistant|lg_tv_lineup", "assistant|lg_tv_deal", "assistant|lg_tv_recommendation", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_picture_quality", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_viewing_angles", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_hdr", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_processor", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_gaming", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_ai_image_enhancement", "assistant|lg_oled_c9_audio"], "digest_bytes": 6303}
{"question_id": "95228167", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your profile, here's what to focus on during your store visit:\n\n**Try both guitars side by side.** You currently play a Fender Stratocaster and are considering a Gibson Les Paul — play both before deciding, and pay attention to which one feels and sounds like \"home\" to you.\n\n**Test the coil-tap/coil-split feature.** You wanted extra tonal flexibility, especially for open D tuning. Ask to try:\n- Gibson Les Paul Standard (coil-tap on the tone controls)\n- Gibson Les Paul Traditional (coil-splitting)\n- Gibson Les Paul Custom (often coil-tap or coil-split)\n\n**Bring your own test material.** Since you're into acoustic-like fingerpicking (e.g., the \"Blackbird\" intro), actually try that style on each guitar. Keep in mind the Les Paul is generally better suited to rock, blues, and metal than delicate fingerpicking — so test how it handles that style for you specifically. Using the neck pickup and a lighter touch will help get closer to that acoustic feel.\n\n**Check the setup for open D.** If the store allows, ask about heavier string gauges (0.012–0.052 or 0.013–0.056) for a fuller, more resonant open D tone, and notice how the low register feels and sounds.\n\n**Comfort check.** Feel the neck, weight, and balance — the Les Paul is notably heavier than your Strat, so make sure it's comfortable for longer playing sessions.", "n_records": 13, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 2483, "completion_tokens": 1698, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|current_guitar", "self|guitar_interest_open_d_tuning", "self|playing_interest_fingerpicking", "self|guitar_feature_preference", "assistant|guitar_purchase_advice", "assistant|open_d_setup_strings", "assistant|open_d_setup_guitar", "assistant|open_d_amp_settings", "assistant|open_d_pickup_advice", "assistant|open_d_technique_advice", "assistant|les_paul_coil_tap_models", "assistant|les_paul_fingerpicking_tips", "assistant|les_paul_genre_fit"